{"name":"Conceptual","hex":"#7ac34f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"conceptual","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, or relating to concepts or mental conception.","Existing only in the imagination."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/conceptual"}],"entries":[{"title":"Concept","blocks":[{"text":"A concept is a fundamental unit of cognition that classifies entities and encodes shared features. Concepts make it possible to form and combine ideas, draw inferences, and refer to external objects. They act as the meanings of words and play a central role in many cognitive processes, including perception, memory, and reasoning. Researchers distinguish different types of concepts based on their internal structure, mode of acquisition, and domain. These include simple and complex concepts, learned and innate concepts, concrete and abstract concepts, and natural and logical concepts."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concept","wikidata":"Q151885"},{"title":"Conceptualism","blocks":[{"text":"In metaphysics, conceptualism is a theory that explains universality of particulars as conceptualized frameworks situated within the thinking mind. Intermediate between nominalism and realism, the conceptualist view approaches the metaphysical concept of universals from a perspective that denies their presence in particulars outside the mind's perception of them. Conceptualism is anti-realist about abstract objects, just like immanent realism is."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Abelard.jpg/330px-Abelard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conceptualism","wikidata":"Q848969"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/500px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/1280px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg/500px-CHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACHEZY_QUEVANNE_Portrait_of_a_mother_and_her_son%2C_1805.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/500px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/1280px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg/500px-Ludwig_Wittgenstein.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ludwig Wittgenstein challenged the classical theory, arguing that concepts are usually based on family resemblance without precise underlying definitions.[35]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALudwig_Wittgenstein.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg/500px-Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg/500px-Jerry_Fodor_in_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jerry Fodor proposed conceptual atomism, arguing that lexical concepts are simple units without internal structure.[49]","link":"h